---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../consts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How we research and write"
  description="What Fader Report does and does not do: no hands-on testing, every price dated, AI used in drafting under human oversight, and corrections published openly."
>
  <h1 class="pagetitle">How we research and write</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The useful version of this page is the list of things we cannot do. Here it is first.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>We do not test products by hand.</strong> We own no test rig and no plugin
      licences. We never write “we tested”, “in our testing” or “after weeks of use”, because
      none of that would be true. When a comparison judges sound quality, the judgement belongs
      to a named third party and is linked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We do not invent people.</strong> No stock-photo reviewers, no fictional
      engineers with twenty years in the industry, no made-up credentials.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We do not invent numbers.</strong> No estimated market sizes, no “studies show”
      without the study. If we could not verify a figure, the article says the figure could not
      be verified rather than quietly rounding a guess.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We do not publish undated prices.</strong> A price with no date is a price you
      cannot trust.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How a piece gets made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      A topic is chosen because people are trying to make a specific decision and the existing
      results answer it badly — usually because they are stale, or written by the vendor.
    </li>
    <li>
      Primary sources are fetched and read: terms of service, pricing pages, official policy
      documents, source repositories. Secondary sources are used only where a primary one does
      not exist, and are named in the text.
    </li>
    <li>
      The piece is drafted, then fact-checked claim by claim against those sources. Anything
      that cannot be traced to a source is cut, not softened.
    </li>
    <li>Every source is listed at the bottom with the date it was read.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Our use of AI, stated plainly</h2>
  <p>
    Research and drafting on this site are done with AI assistance. We think you are entitled
    to know that, so we are telling you rather than hoping you do not ask.
  </p>
  <p>
    What that does not change: every factual claim is checked against a source that was
    actually retrieved, and those sources are listed so you can check them too. What it does
    change: you should treat the source list as the real product and the prose as our reading
    of it. If our reading and the source disagree, the source wins, and we want to hear about
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Articles carry a <strong>Fader Report editorial team</strong> byline for the writing, and
    name a reviewer where a person has read the piece and signed off on it. Fader Report is
    edited by <strong>Arnau Grani Romero</strong>, and his name appears as reviewer on the
    articles he has checked. An article with no named reviewer has not had one — we would
    rather you could tell the difference at a glance than assume a level of oversight that
    was not there.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing and licence maintenance</h2>
  <p>
    Prices and licence terms in this niche move constantly, so pages carry the date their
    facts were verified and are scheduled for re-checking roughly every 30 days. When we
    update a page we change the “updated” date. A page whose dates have gone stale is a
    liability, and we would rather you could see that at a glance than be quietly misled.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong we fix it and note that we did. We do not silently edit an error
    out of existence. <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>Report a mistake</a> — a correction is
    more useful to us than praise, and we mean that literally.
  </p>

  <h2>Money</h2>
  <p>
    The site currently earns nothing. No affiliate links, no advertising, no sponsored posts,
    no paid placement, ever. If and when affiliate links are added, they will be disclosed on
    every page that carries them, before the content, and nothing about our recommendations
    will be for sale. The full policy is on the
    <a href={withBase('/affiliate-disclosure/')}>affiliate disclosure page</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
